    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in high-acid environments. Can you explain the specific molecular mechanics of how lecithin acts as an emulsifier in this context, especially regarding the oil-water interface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin acts as a molecular bridge at the oil-water interface, with its hydrophilic head groups anchoring into the acidic aqueous phase while its hydrophobic tails embed themselves in the fat globules. This amphiphilic structure creates a stable monolayer that reduces interfacial tension, preventing the droplets from coalescing under high-acid stress. However, in high-acid environments, the protonation of lecithin's polar groups can alter its conformation and reduce electrostatic repulsion between droplets, leading to the phase separation you're observing. To mitigate this, you might consider adding a small amount of a cationic emulsifier or adjusting the pH slightly to optimize the charge density of the lecithin head groups.\n\nYou might also want to check if your specific lecithin source (soy, egg yolk, sunflower) has different stability profiles in acidic conditions, as some are more prone to oxidation or hydrolysis. A little bit of a gummy texture in your final product could be a sign of incomplete emulsification or the presence of undissolved lecithin particles, which might be acting as nucleation sites for separation.\n\nSpeaking of which,",
      "source": "target",
