    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The protonation of lecithin's polar head groups (specifically the phosphate and carboxylate groups) in a high-acid environment neutralizes their negative charge. This loss of surface charge eliminates the electrostatic repulsion that normally keeps the oil droplets apart, allowing them to coalesce. As the repulsive barrier drops, the interfacial tension effectively increases because the surfactant layer becomes disordered and less effective at stabilizing the interface, causing the droplets to merge into larger, unstable phases.\n\nThink of it like a crowd of people holding hands in a circle to block a wall; if you take away their ability to hold hands (the charge), they naturally drift apart and let the wall crumble. In your mayo, the \"wall\" of repulsion holding the oil droplets suspended in the acid is gone, so gravity and Brownian motion take over, causing the separation.\n\nTo fix this, you could try using a lecithin with a higher pKa value or adding a small amount of a salt that doesn't interfere with the emulsion, though sometimes a slight pH adjustment is the most efficient solution. It's a delicate balance, much like trying to keep a goblin from breaking a seal",
